cs, and the partisan baseline points to a double-digit Republican win. The incumbent president is term-limited, so neither party benefits from incumbency, and the national environment currently reads as neutral. No statewide general-election polls, expert ratings, or market prices are available, so the forecast leans entirely on fundamentals.\n\nWithout fresh polling, uncertainty is wide, but there is little in the evidence to suggest Mississippi will be close. A Democratic upset would require a substantial national wave well beyond anything currently indicated. The Republican ticket is favored, with a modest chance of a closer-than-expected result if the national climate turns sharply against the party holding the state.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1069, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1631, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.153, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1578, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.153, "margin": -11.0}]}